## Key Learnings & Design Insights

### 1. Stability is the Central LDO Design Challenge
Achieving phase margin > 45° at realistic on-chip load 
capacitance (100pF) required a three-part compensation 
strategy — increasing Miller capacitor, adding a 
feedforward zero (CFF), and introducing an ESR zero 
(RESR). No single technique was sufficient alone. 
Phase margin improved from <10° to 72.75° through 
iterative compensation tuning.

### 2. GBW Must Be Controlled, Not Maximized
The OTA used was designed for a GBW of ~933 MHz. 
In the closed LDO loop, this caused the unity gain 
frequency to exceed 500 MHz — this is where internal poles 
degraded phase margin. Reducing GBW by increasing Cc 
brought UGF to 128.5 MHz, improving both phase margin 
and gain margin simultaneously. Higher bandwidth is not 
always better in feedback systems.

### 3. Simulation Artifacts vs Real Results
Several misleading results were encountered:
- NaN in STB → iprobe placement issue, not instability
- Flat line in noise → wrong output node or missing AC source  
- xxx in ADE outputs → analysis type mismatch
- −6400 dB PSRR → expression evaluated in wrong analysis
Learning to distinguish simulation setup errors from 
real circuit problems is as important as circuit design.

### 4. C_LOAD Choice Affects Credibility
Using 100 nF load capacitance gave excellent phase margin 
but is not physically realizable on TSMC N7. Reducing to 
100 pF (realistic on-chip MIM cap) exposed the true 
stability challenge and led to the compensation work. 

### 5. PSRR Bandwidth is Limited by UGF
Supply rejection is only effective within the loop 
bandwidth. PSRR measured −52.26 dB up to ~2 MHz, 
degrading beyond the UGF of 128.5 MHz. Improving PSRR 
at high frequencies requires either higher UGF (risks 
stability) or feedforward supply rejection paths — a 
known architectural tradeoff in LDO design.

### 6. Process Corners Validate the Design
Simulating across VDD (0.8V–1.0V) and temperature 
(−40°C to 125°C) confirmed phase margin never dropped 
below 52° and PSRR remained better than −29 dB across 
all conditions. Corner analysis is not optional — 
a design that works only at typical conditions is not 
a real design.
